# Chroma Library, modern Earth: shadow moments (next version, after v22). Library thread, 2026-10-07.
# Spec: chroma-philosophy/shadows.md (Emren chose "Shadows", 12:18 UTC 10-07) and colour-canon.md section 2.
# A colour that rules a life shows its own weakness. The engine holds five states, read like the 21 adjectives:
#   rigid (White), indecisive (Blue), ruthless (Black), reckless (Red), stuck in their ways (Green).
# Each moment needs its state (holds:, compiled to the engine's requires), so only people in that state meet it.
# One moment per colour per stage from young adult on (20). Every moment has the five one-colour options and one
# cross cycle; the cycles s1-s4 are used five times each, so the part is colour-even on its own.
# What the shadow lacks comes through the engine's fail_fit: alpha calls the colour's two opposites (.3 each),
# its two neighbours (.15) and itself (.1), so a failure while doubling down teaches what was missing.
# Chances: the shadow's own act 40, a neighbour's 62, an opposite's 66; cross options by their means colour
# (own 52, neighbour 58, opposite 62). Every colour holds each role equally often in every stage.
# No colour is named in player text; no option is called wrong. Engine reading for Red (10-07): reckless is risks
# that cost (bridges burned, the body paying); impulsive is acting before thinking.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- rigid (White)

== the house rota, again
tier: life event
stages: young_adult
age: 18-30
holds: rigid
per_year: 0 0 .3 0 0 0
gap: 2-5
alpha: W.1 U.15 B.3 R.3 G.15
times: an estimate: about one person in three who is rigid meets it in a year as a young adult, while sharing a home; it comes back now and then for as long as the state holds (shadows.md)
likelier: a shared flat or house; a first home with a partner; a job with a strict routine
rarer: living alone; a house where someone else keeps order
tone: trouble
life: home, friends
horizon: months
roles: friend
earth: in a shared house the rota has broken down again, and {N} is the only one who still keeps it
scene: The bins have not gone out for the third week. {N} wrote the rota, laminated it and pinned it by the kettle, and {N} is the only one who still keeps to it. {friend} has started eating out to avoid the kitchen.
scene W: {N} cannot see why a promise on paper should mean less than one said aloud. The rota was agreed, and everyone signed it.
- W | call a house meeting and read the rota out, line by line, until everyone signs again | v: conformity, universalism | mark: made an enemy | chance: 40
- U | work out a fairer rota from who is home when, and try it for a month | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 62
- B | stop doing anyone else's share, and let the mess land where it falls | v: self-direction, power | chance: 66
- R | leave the rota alone, cook a big dinner for everyone, and talk of other things | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 66
- G | let it go for a while, and see what the house settles into on its own | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 62
- W>U | keep the rule, but ask each housemate what would make it work for them | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 52
- U>B | price the cleaning out, and suggest everyone pays a cleaner instead | v: achievement, security | chance: 58
- B>R | swap the jobs you hate for the ones you don't mind, and enjoy the bargain | v: hedonism, power | chance: 62
- R>G | turn Sunday cleaning into a house ritual with loud music and a fry-up | v: stimulation, tradition | chance: 62
- G>W | quietly do a little extra yourself, as an example rather than a lecture | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 58
works: The house finds a rhythm, and {friend} is back in the kitchen by the end of the month.
works: The rota fades from the fridge, and nobody misses it much.
fails: {friend} finds a room somewhere else and moves out at the end of the month.
fails: The kitchen turns into a cold war of labelled shelves and notes on the fridge.

== a rule bent for a good reason
tier: life event
at: employer
stages: adult
age: 26-45
holds: rigid
per_year: 0 0 0 .3 0 0
gap: 2-5
alpha: W.1 U.15 B.3 R.3 G.15
times: an estimate: about one person in three who is rigid meets it in a year of adult working life; it comes back now and then for as long as the state holds (shadows.md)
likelier: a job with customers and fees; a workplace with a thick rule book
rarer: working alone; being the one in charge of the rules
tone: mixed
life: work, conscience
horizon: weeks
roles: colleague, boss
earth: a colleague bent a rule to help someone, and {N} is the one who saw it
scene: {colleague} let an old customer off a late fee, against the rules, because the woman had just buried her husband. {N} saw it, and {colleague} knows {N} saw it.
scene W: A rule bent once for a good reason is a rule bent twice for a worse one. {N} has watched it happen before.
- W | report it to {boss}, as the rules say you must | v: conformity, security | mark: made an enemy | chance: 40
- U | look up why the rule exists, and whether there is a proper way to waive it | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 62
- B | say nothing, and remember that {colleague} owes you one | v: power, security | chance: 66
- R | tell {colleague} you would have done the same, and buy them a drink after work | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 66
- G | let it pass: some kindnesses are older than rules | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 62
- W>B | have a quiet word with {colleague}, so it stays on record between the two of you | v: conformity, power | chance: 52
- U>R | draft a hardship rule that lets staff use their own judgement, and push it through | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 58
- B>G | tidy the paperwork, so the widow keeps her break and the team keeps its good name | v: power, benevolence | chance: 62
- R>W | tell {boss} yourself, hotly, that the rule is the thing that is wrong | v: universalism, stimulation | mark: defied an authority | chance: 62
- G>U | ask the old hands how such cases were handled before the rule was written | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 58
works: The matter closes quietly, and {colleague} trusts {N} a little more than before.
works: Months later the rule is changed, and nobody remembers who started it.
fails: {colleague} stops talking to {N}, and the team takes sides.
fails: It comes out anyway, and {N} is the one people blame for the fuss.

== everyone's first call
tier: life event
stages: mature
age: 40-65
holds: rigid
per_year: 0 0 0 0 .3 0
gap: 2-5
alpha: W.1 U.15 B.3 R.3 G.15
times: an estimate: about one person in three who is rigid meets it in a year of midlife, when duties pile up from every side; it comes back now and then for as long as the state holds (shadows.md)
likelier: ageing parents; a club, a faith or a charity that leans on a few people; being the eldest
rarer: a partner or siblings who share the load; a job that leaves no evenings
tone: trouble
life: duty, family, health
horizon: months
roles: parent, friend, sibling
earth: {N} is the one everyone calls, and there is nothing left over for {N}
scene: The treasurer's job at the club, the lifts for {parent}, the rota at the food bank, {sibling}'s paperwork. {N} said yes to all of it, and tonight the phone rings again.
scene W: {N} was raised to believe that anyone who can help must. Nobody ever said where it stops.
- W | say yes again: someone has to, and it is always you | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 40
- U | write down every duty and the hours it takes, and decide what can go | v: self-direction, security | chance: 62
- B | say no, plainly, and keep one evening a week that belongs to nobody but you | v: self-direction, hedonism | self_control: + | chance: 66
- R | switch the phone off and go to the coast for the weekend, alone | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 66
- G | let the club and the food bank find their own way for a season | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 62
- W>R | hand the treasurer's job on, as the rules allow, and take up something for joy | v: conformity, hedonism | chance: 52
- U>G | train two others in the work, so the help can go on in other hands | v: universalism, benevolence | chance: 58
- B>W | ask {sibling} to pay a fair share for the help, so the load is split evenly | v: power, universalism | chance: 62
- R>U | book a week's course in something you always wanted to learn, and go | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 62
- G>B | step back to your own house and garden for a while, and let that be enough | v: security, self-direction | chance: 58
works: The calls slow down, the world keeps turning, and {N} sleeps through the night again.
works: Others step in, and {N} finds that some of the duties were never really needed.
fails: {N} keeps going until the body forces a stop, and a doctor says the word exhaustion.
fails: Every gap {N} leaves fills with guilt, and by the spring {N} has said yes to all of it again.

== the family does things differently now
tier: life event
stages: elder
age: 60-95
holds: rigid
needs: children
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 .3
gap: 2-5
alpha: W.1 U.15 B.3 R.3 G.15
times: an estimate: about one person in three who is rigid and has grown children meets it in a year of old age; it comes back now and then for as long as the state holds (shadows.md)
likelier: grandchildren; a family that gathers at {Ns} table; children who married into other ways
rarer: children far away; a family that rarely gathers
tone: mixed
life: family, home
horizon: years
roles: child
earth: at a family gathering, {Ns} grown children do everything differently, and {N} says so
scene: Sunday lunch at {Ns} table. {child} lets the little ones eat on the sofa, nobody says grace, and the phones stay on. {N} has said something about it every visit this year, and the visits are getting shorter.
scene W: {N} kept this family together with a few plain rules. It is hard to watch them dropped as if they never mattered.
- W | say it once more, clearly: in this house, there are rules | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 40
- U | ask {child} why they do things their way, and listen to the answer | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 62
- B | stop hosting, keep your own days full, and let them come when they want | v: self-direction, hedonism | chance: 66
- R | sit on the floor with the grandchildren and let lunch be loud | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 66
- G | let each generation raise its own, as it always was | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 62
- W>G | keep one rule only, grace before the meal, and let everything else go | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 52
- U>W | write the family's rules and recipes into a book for the grandchildren, to keep or ignore | v: universalism, tradition | chance: 58
- B>U | strike a deal with the eldest grandchild: you teach them chess, they teach you the phone | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 62
- R>B | announce that you are off travelling this summer, and see who misses whom | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 62
- G>R | take the little ones out to the garden, and let them get muddy the old way | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 58
works: The visits get longer again, and {child} starts asking {N} for advice.
works: {N} finds the house louder and happier than any rule ever made it.
fails: {child} starts finding reasons not to come, and Sunday lunch is set for two.
fails: A sharp word at the table is repeated in the car home, and the next visit is a long time coming.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- indecisive (Blue)

== an answer needed by Friday
tier: life event
stages: young_adult
age: 18-30
holds: indecisive
per_year: 0 0 .3 0 0 0
gap: 2-5
alpha: W.15 U.1 B.15 R.3 G.3
times: an estimate: about one person in three who is indecisive meets it in a year as a young adult, the years of offers with deadlines; it comes back now and then for as long as the state holds (shadows.md)
likelier: the last year of school or study; looking for a first job; a partner who wants to move
rarer: a settled job; a family that decides for them
tone: trouble
life: work, study, future
horizon: weeks
roles: friend, mentor
earth: an offer must be answered by Friday, and {N} is still comparing
scene: The offer came on Monday: a place on the course, or the job in another town, if {N} answers by Friday. It is Thursday night, and {N} has three spreadsheets and no answer.
scene U: Every option has a column, every column has a weight, and the weights keep changing. One more night of thinking could settle it.
- U | ask for another week to weigh it properly | v: self-direction, security | mark: turned down a chance | chance: 40
- W | ask {mentor} what someone in your place ought to do, and do it | v: conformity, security | chance: 62
- B | take whichever pays more, and keep your options open for later | v: achievement, power | chance: 62
- R | say yes tonight, before the doubt can catch up | v: stimulation, self-direction | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 66
- G | go with what your gut said on Monday, before the spreadsheets | v: self-direction, security | chance: 66
- W>B | keep the half promise to go with {friend}, and make it work | v: conformity, achievement | chance: 58
- U>R | give yourself one hour and a coin: whatever you feel when it lands is the answer | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 52
- B>G | ring them and bargain for a later start, to stay near home a little longer | v: power, tradition | chance: 58
- R>W | ring {mentor} at midnight, say your heart is set, and ask to be held to it | v: stimulation, conformity | chance: 62
- G>U | walk the long way home, and write the answer down when it comes | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 62
works: The answer goes in on time, and {N} wonders why it took so long.
works: Whatever {N} chose, {N} is busy living it by the autumn.
fails: Friday comes and goes, and the place goes to someone on the waiting list.
fails: {N} answers at the last minute, half sure, and spends a year wondering.

