rarer: little to leave; a will settled long ago
tone: trouble
life: family, money
horizon: years
roles: child
earth: {N} has been using the will to keep the family close
scene: {N} has changed the will twice this year, and everyone knows it. {child} visits on Sundays, polite and careful, and leaves early. The house is full of things and very quiet.
scene B: It is {Ns} money, earned by {N} alone. If it buys a little attention, that is what money is for.
- B | change the will again, to whoever came most this year | v: power | chance: 40
- W | divide everything equally, tell them so, and never mention it again | v: universalism, conformity | chance: 66
- U | sit down with {child} and explain what you have and why you planned it so | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 62
- R | spend a good part of it now, on a trip with the whole family | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 62
- G | give the house to the grandchildren now, while you can watch them live in it | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 66
- W>B | settle the will fairly, and keep enough in your own name to live as you please | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 62
- U>R | fund a grandchild's first big adventure, and ask to hear all about it | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 58
- B>G | set up a family trust, and keep the running of it while you live | v: power, tradition | chance: 52
- R>W | tell {child} you have been unfair, and say sorry before the lawyer does anything else | v: benevolence, universalism | mark: owned up | chance: 58
- G>U | pay for a grandchild's studies, quietly, and ask only for a letter now and then | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 62
works: The visits grow longer, and nobody mentions the will.
